  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 117 PM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nAs the aforementioned upper low begins to eject into the Dakotas on \nWednesday, an associated surface low will move quickly east across \nSouth Dakota Wednesday afternoon. This drags a surface cold front \nthrough the area from west to east, clearing the area by Wednesday \nevening. Strengthening west winds are expected in the wake of this \nfront, as the downslope flow promotes ample mixing to tap into \nhigher momentum flow aloft. Westerly wind gusts as high as 40 to 50 \nmiles per hour can be expected, and will also coincide with humidity \nfalling into the 30 to 40 percent range. This could lead to elevated \nfire concerns, and trends will continue to be monitored.  \n\nShortwave ridging then quickly moves overhead on Tuesday, before \nattention then turns to a deep upper low beginning to move east into \nthe Rockies on Friday. Guidance still largely remains at odds with \nthe exact evolution of this system, though better agreement is noted \nwith this morning's guidance suite. Deterministic solutions now \nsuggest a somewhat weaker upper low, with it remaining an open wave \nas it crosses the Dakotas by Saturday morning. This further north \ntrack also points towards lesser cold air locally, and would suggest \nthe area largely remains in the warm sector. In fact, some guidance \nhas hinted at thunderstorm development Friday evening along the cold \nfront, and even a low threat for a stronger storm or two given \nstrong shear and weak instability. Should the further north and \nsomewhat weaker evolution occur, this would also keep the gusty \nwinds somewhat weaker as well, though ensemble guidance still shows \nbroad low probabilities for gusts >40mph. Confidence will remain low \nuntil details become more clear, though is slowly increasing with \nrespect to less wintry precipitation and towards a threat for \nshowers and thunderstorms Friday evening into Saturday. The system \nthen departs Sunday, with cooler highs expected across the area in \nits wake.",
